# Shopify vs repository (HARD — non-negotiable)

## Source of truth

**The repository is the Design System.** GitHub / **`shopify-build/`** owns sections, snippets, assets, CSS, JS, and docs.

Shopify is **only the runtime** for testing — never the master. Do **not** develop primarily in Theme Editor. Do **not** treat a remote theme as source of truth. Any fix found in QA → revise in **repo first**, then re-push to draft if needed.

## Workflow

1. Build in repository  
2. Commit (GitHub master)  
3. QA push to a **disposable draft** — **only** when Andrew names a theme ID in that same message (ask if missing; never invent)  
4. Revise in repository  
5. Repeat until approved  
6. **Brian** pulls approved repo → production theme  

Draft themes are temporary and may be deleted anytime.

## Default: no Shopify mutations

Agents **MUST NOT** run `shopify theme push`, `pull`, `dev`, `delete`, publish, rename, or Theme Editor API changes unless Andrew **explicitly names a theme ID in that same message**.

## Retired / dead draft

- Theme ID **`187143618851`** is **retired / deleted** — do **not** use it.
- Fail closed. Never guess a replacement. New disposable ID must be explicitly provided.

## Forbidden without explicit in-message approval

- Live / published theme (any Current)
- Any Theme library copy, backup, duplicate, or “backcopy” used as master
- Local donor `/Users/andrewnehra/barreletics-theme-live-apr2026` as a push target (read-only reference only)
- Publishing any theme
- Any Shopify CLI theme command without a theme ID from Andrew in the current message

## If ambiguous

STOP and ask for a disposable draft theme ID. Never invent. Never push “to be safe.”

## Production integration

Homepage / Theme Editor assembly and production integration = **Brian** from the approved repository — not agents treating Shopify as the Design System.
